MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

All three are shipped, default-off, and `unevaluated` in the flag ledger. None
had any queued path to the evidence that would promote or retire it. The plans
existed only in ops/status.md prose, and the planner reads ops/known-issues.md
MANDATORY-NEXT-MILESTONE as its task source, not status.md — so the plans could
not reach a roadmap.

Queued as ONE entry because they are one dependency chain: tool use needs the
actions-to-progress result to justify a kernel flip; the supervisor's fourth arm
is gated on that same evidence; and tool-gap generation needs live runs with the
loop ON to have anything to analyse.

The cheapest unlock is the supervisor window. `_make_trajectory_supervisor`
defaults CARNOT_ARC_TRAJECTORY_SUPERVISOR_WINDOW to 400, which IS the action
budget, so a stagnation window essentially cannot complete before a run ends --
the refinement tool needs 10 firings per arm, has 2, and will report INSUFFICIENT
EVIDENCE forever at this setting. It costs nothing scored to fix: the supervisor
runs in SHADOW mode on every run, so a lower window fills the ledger without
changing scored behaviour.

TWO CORRECTIONS OF MY OWN, recorded rather than quietly dropped:

I claimed status.md's What's Next was "stale by 47 milestones". It was not. I read
a historical block under a 2026-08-27 prior-update header as if it were current;
the file is append-at-top with history preserved per never-prune, and its top
block was accurate.

I claimed zero supervisor redirects had ever fired. Also wrong: I scanned
results/*.json and missed ops/arc_supervisor_refinement_ledger.json, which is what
the refinement tool actually reads -- 9 receipts, 6 redirects, 2 fired / 1 helped
per arm. Below an evidence floor is a different and better state than empty, and
the distinction changes what the next step is.

The entry ends with an instruction not to close any of the three by enabling a
flag. Each is default-off deliberately; the point is to measure before trusting,
not to ship on a gate that only proved the mechanism runs.

